H12 attempted: separating the int8 GEMM's operands into different banks changes its
wall time by less than this machine can resolve
2026-09-09. Desktop 2 (Phoenix, Ryzen 7 8700G), native Windows, mlir-aie ironenv (v1.4.2,
llvm-aie 22.0.0.2026090201) + XRT SDK, power mode Default (1.7983 GHz per
results/aie/clock_probe_npu.log). This run DID take a hardware context. `xrt-smi examine -r
aie-partitions` reported "No hardware contexts running on device" before it, and
tools/host_load.ps1 reported HOST_LOAD_VERDICT CLEAR, though a peer session held about one
core throughout and the spread below reflects that.

Command (from the repo root, ironenv, XRT SDK on PATH and pyxrt on PYTHONPATH):
  python kernels/bank_placement/bank_ab_test.py --repeats 4 --iters 60 --warmup 5

WHAT WAS PREDICTED, AND BY WHOM. results/aie/bank_conflict_survey.log found the production
whole_array int8 GEMM landing both input tiles in bank 2 with bank 3 empty, and one
paired-load bundle inside its nine-bundle hardware loop -- the situation measured elsewhere to
cost one extra cycle per iteration. results/aie/gemm_cost_model_nest.log put the core's issuing
cost at 2,442 cycles per call against 3,274 measured, i.e. issuing about 75% of the time, and
proposed that a per-buffer floor set by data delivery is what caps the design. H12 was the test
that separates the two readings:

  H12. Placing A and B in different banks will NOT change the measured NPU time, because the
       core is not the critical path. Fails if the dispatch speeds up by about the 3% the
       issuing-cost change predicts (96 cycles of 3,274), which would mean the design is
       issue-bound after all.

THE LEVER, AND WHY IT IS CLEAN. The allocator lays a core's local buffers immediately above its
stack, so raising `stack_size` shifts every buffer up by the same amount and changes nothing
else: same kernel source, same tile shapes, same ObjectFifo depths, same DMA, same schedule.
The compiled kernel object is byte-identical in both arms -- its cache filename carries a
content hash and both builds produced matmul_i8_i32_86901378.o. Only the addresses moved.

NOTE ON PROVENANCE. kernels/bank_placement/whole_array_bankpad.py is a LOCAL COPY of
mlir-aie's whole_array.py, taken while the shared checkout carried only this repo's known
--c-single-buffer patch, with a --stack-size option added. The shared ~/mlir-aie checkout was
NOT modified by this work, deliberately, because another session was working in it.

