--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. READING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE EFFECT IS NOT RESOLVABLE ON THIS MACHINE, AND THAT IS THE RESULT. SEVEN series were run
this sitting. In every one the two arms overlap, and the SIGN OF THE DIFFERENCE CHANGES BETWEEN
SERIES. Best-to-best, in the order run:

    -4.6%   -2.9%   +2.5%   -2.7%   -0.4%   -5.8%   -1.1%

The last two are the ones printed above; the other five are reported here so the set is
complete rather than filtered. A positive number means separating the banks was faster. The
mean is about -2%, the range spans 8 points, and no series reaches the +3% the issue-bound
reading predicts. Anyone quoting a single series would get a different answer depending which
they picked, which is the reason for running seven.

WHAT IS EXCLUDED, AND WHAT IS NOT. The colliding arm's OWN floor across the three earlier
series was 7,638.5, 7,398.9 and 7,275.6 microseconds, a 5.0% drift between repeats of the
IDENTICAL build, which is larger than the ~3% effect being looked for (96 cycles of a
3,274-cycle call). So this measurement excludes a large speedup -- nothing like a 10% gain is
hiding here -- but it cannot separate +3% from 0%. H12 is therefore NOT confirmed and NOT
refuted. It is untested at the resolution this machine allowed today, with a peer session
holding about a core throughout.

WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT, AND IT IS AN INSTRUMENT THIS BRANCH ALREADY HAS. Wall time is the wrong
observable for a 3% core-side change on a shared machine. The trace unit is not: point
kernels/pmu_probe/'s event routing at one core of this design and read ACTIVE against
LOCK_STALL directly in core cycles, inside the dispatch, where host contention cannot reach.
If the floor is real, the separated build's issuing cycles fall by about 96 per call and its
lock stall rises by the same amount, leaving ACTIVE unchanged -- an equality that needs no
timing at all. The obstacle is known and named in RESEARCH.md: the upstream whole_array design
carries no trace hook, so one has to be added to the local copy first.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT RETRACT. The bank penalty itself is not in question here. It is measured
elsewhere and predicted exactly on a kernel that is compute-bound
(results/aie/bank_check_validation.log: 1,024 cycles per panel, predicted and measured, on a
design whose compute sits in a software loop with one paired load per MAC). What this run
tried and failed to resolve is the narrower question of whether that penalty is VISIBLE in the
wall time of this particular whole-array design, where the cost model says the core finishes
early and waits.

A VALIDITY CHECK WORTH RECORDING. The colliding arm is a local copy of the design driven
through a new script, so it has to reproduce the original result. Its best time this sitting,
7,275.6 microseconds, taken in one of the three earlier series, sits just under the 2026-09-07
sweep's 7,359.9 microsecond minimum for the identical shape and tile. The control reproduces,
and the harness is not introducing a systematic offset.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THIS DOES NOT SHOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- One shape (4096x2048x2048), one tile (m=64 k=64 n=64), one dtype, one power mode, four
  columns, one machine, one sitting.
- A peer session held roughly one core throughout. The verdict was CLEAR by this repo's own
  gate and the NPU had no foreign context, but the spread is far wider than the 2026-09-07
  sweep's 3.7%, reaching 82% in the noisiest run, and every number here should be read that
  way. This experiment should be repeated on a quiet machine before anyone leans on it.
- No mechanism is claimed for either direction of the observed differences. They are within
  the drift.
- The intervention was verified in the first of 16 core ELFs. Allocation is per core and the
  other 15 were not checked.
- The conclusion that the core is not the critical path REMAINS AN INFERENCE from the cost
  model, not something this run established. That was the point of the test, and the test did
  not have the resolution to deliver it.
